<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tawagalawa letter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tawagalawa letter is a fragmentary Hittite diplomatic letter of the 13th century BCE, usually attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> and addressed to the king of Ahhiyawa, whom it calls 'brother'. It complains about the raids of the renegade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piyamaradu">Piyamaradu</a>, who used Millawanda (Miletus) as a base, and asks the Ahhiyawan king to hand him over. It is important evidence for relations between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and the Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tawagalawa-letter.jpg" alt="Tawagalawa letter" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Zunkir · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moyen_Orient_Amarna_1.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
